CHAPTER XI

COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 19

1. In providing protection for women and children victims of violence, the community may:a. form family partners at the village/kelurahan level in coordination with Desa Pakraman;
b. form women and child protection units within community organizations;
c. conduct independent socialization of women's and children's rights;
d. provide first aid to victims; and e. report to the competent authorities if violence against victims occurs in their environment.
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by individuals, Desa Pakraman, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, the private sector, and mass media.

Article 11

1. The MSS as referred to in Article 10 has performance indicators including:a. coverage of women and children victims of violence who receive complaint handling by trained officers within the integrated service unit;
b. coverage of women and children victims of violence who receive health services by trained health personnel at Community Health Centers and Hospitals:c. coverage of social rehabilitation services provided by trained social rehabilitation officers for women and children victims of violence within the integrated service unit:d. coverage of spiritual guidance services provided by trained spiritual guidance officers for women and children victims of violence within the integrated service unit:e. coverage of law enforcement from the investigation level to court decisions on cases of violence against women and children;
f. coverage of women and children victims of violence who receive legal assistance services;
g. coverage of repatriation services for women and children victims of violence; andh. coverage of social reintegration services for women and children victims of violence.
2. The determination of MSS performance indicators for Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence as referred to in paragraph (1) constitutes a minimum target that must be achieved by the integrated service unit in stages.

CHAPTER VI

MINIMUM SERVICE STANDARDS

Article 10

1. The MSS for Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence aims to ensure that women and children victims of violence receive the minimum services needed.
2. The MSS for Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence includes services for:a. handling complaints/reports of victims of violence against women and children;
b. health services for women and children victims of violence;
c. social rehabilitation for women and children victims of violence;
d. law enforcement and legal assistance for women and children victims of violence;
e. repatriation and social reintegration for women and children victims of violence; andf. in the event that victims experience trauma or illness that endangers themselves as a result of acts of violence requiring immediate assistance, the regional government is obligated to provide first aid no later than 7 (seven) days after the complaint is filed.

CHAPTER III

RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

Article 4

The rights of victims include:
a. protection from the family;
b. protection from the police;
c. protection from the prosecutor's office;
d. protection from the court;
e. protection from advocates,f. protection from social institutions, or other parties either temporarily or based on the determination of a protection order from the court;
g. health services in accordance with medical needs; h. special handling related to victim confidentiality;
i. assistance by social workers and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
j. spiritual guidance services; andk. victims have the right to determine their own decisions regarding which option will be implemented.

CHAPTER X

GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

Article 17

1. The Regent conducts guidance on the development of PPT. (2) Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) includes: a. provision of implementation instructions;
b. mentoring;
c. supervision;
d. monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of services for women and children victims of violence, both apparatus and community.

